or our software to look at  so we're going to want to use that for  as many scenes as we can but if frames  are missing or if the decomp and the  damage is so distracting and  irreconcilable we'll switch to the other  nitrate print that we have this nitrate  was sent over from france where pinto  ben was released as le pony de rio gym  some pieces were actually excised  throughout the years maybe because of  d-comp or damage we don't know to fill  in chunks that are still missing the  team will defer to one of three  lower-res prints of the film from a 1924  re-release  altogether these prints contain the most  information since they have the highest  number of frames intact but the image  quality here is probably the roughest  you can see the focus is a little bit  fuzzier  it's a little bit flatter the contrast  isn't as good  they can play with the contrast to help  correct for any obvious quality jumps  between frames and color grading will  help tie everything together and then  from all those different elements and  all those different frames we assemble  as completed version as possible once a  timeline of all the best shots has been  created digital restoration begins the  first thing to correct is this shaky  movement you see between frames often  it's the result of film shrinking over  time shrunken film doesn't go through  the scanner as well so you can end up  with an unstable image we know in like  analog film there's a little bit of a  slight vibration in the film image but  if it were to regularly kind of  distractingly move up and down we would  try and eliminate that to a degree the  thing that this element suffers from is  mostly scratching software helps detect  these but it's not a perfect solution in  this film there's a lot of wallpaper and  a lot of vertical lines that resemble  these types of scratches so i put in a  scratch that's very thick it's going to  say hey i'm this line on the wallpaper  right i should be removing that he can  use the same kind of programmed filters  to help detect dark dirt light dust or  other errant artifacts like the stray  eyelash or hair that may have become  embedded into the print so that's just a  piece of parent dirt that fell you know  onto the film at some point other  artifacts might be captured in the image  itself for example if there was dirt on  the printer or a camera negative when  the film was printed however many years  ago you tend to find the most dirt and  dust at the ends of the film those would  be exposed to the elements more just  sitting in a can would be their heads  out or tails out that would accumulate  dust when you're putting it in the can  taking it out  in general the more popular a film was  and the more times it was shown in  theaters the more likely it is to have  suffered extensive wear and tear from  scratches and thumb prints to spots like  this where the emulsions rubbed off a  common effect of repeated projection  the last step in the process color  grading here the team has the scanned  frames of pinto ben up on the screen and  the nitrate film out on the inspection  table the goal is to make the colors on  that screen match those in the physical  reference as closely as possible this  takes a lot of precision and teamwork  warmer colder i would say warmer but i  feel like we've lost some of the  saturation it's a very deep vivid almost  radioactive yellow  they have to be very gentle with a print  like this as they go through and inspect  the color in each scene as you can see  the film is kind of bending in one  direction as well as these edges here  are kind of buckling out a little bit  those are all signs of warpage and  physical deterioration of the film stock  if that gets any worse this element  might not be able to be scanned in the  future and so when we inspect it we move  through it very slowly  because the last thing we want to do is  to introduce more damage to the film  print  at the end of the process the film looks  like this pretty close to how it was  originally seen by audiences back in  1915  and that's the goal  we're trying to present the film as an  artifact and an art form the way it was  originally presented this whole process  can take up to two years which means  many movies are deteriorating faster  than archives can preserve them  if we have a unique print where we know  that this is the last surviving element  of a film and we notice that it's  starting to decompose that will go to  the top of our preservation priority  list pinto ben is one of nine william s  hart films eastman is restoring and one  of thousands of movies the museums  helped rescue from being lost forever  [Music]  you
 

